Analyze this legacy .NET Framework 4.8 code running inside a Web API controller:
public class UserReportController : ApiController {
    public HttpResponseMessage GetUserData(string userId) {
        var client = new HttpWebRequest();
        var result = FetchDataFromExternalApiAsync(userId).Result;
        return Request.CreateResponse(HttpStatusCode.OK, result);
    }
    private async Task<string> FetchDataFromExternalApiAsync(string id) {
        using (var client = new HttpClient()) {
            var response = await client.GetAsync("https://api.internal/users/" + id);
            return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
        }
    }
}
Highlight the 2 critical architectural dangers when migrating this code to .NET 8/10 (SynchronizationContext deadlocks and HttpClient socket exhaustion) and show the refactored modern version using clean async/await and IHttpClientFactory.
